      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The problem involves multiplication of fractions. We start by identifying the $n^{\\text{th}}$ term of each fraction series. Starting with the first series $\\frac{1}{4}$, we encounter the sequence $\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{2}{5}, \\frac{3}{6}, \\frac{4}{7}$, and so forth through the entire sequence. To compute the sum of all fractions, we will first construct a product that eliminates improper or misplaced terms within the product.\n\nFirst, we multiply the entire fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nGiven that $n^{\\text{th}}$ term in each fraction series formed thus\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{n=50} \\frac{n}{n+3}, \n\\]\n\nthat is, $\\frac{n}{n+3} = \\frac{n}{n} \\cdot \\frac{1}{n+3}$. \n\nHence, the new product can be derived as:\n\\[\n\\prod_{n=1}^{50} \\frac{n}{n+3} = \\frac{1}{1+3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{2+3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{3+3} \\cdots \\frac{50}{50+3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdots \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the new product:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 53}\n\\]\n\nNotice that several terms in the numerator unoppose with corresponding terms in the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{53}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final result can be wrote as:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{53}}\n\\]",
